      "commit_id": null,
      "commit_url": null,
      "created_at": "2026-09-04T15:19:52Z",
      "updated_at": "2026-09-04T15:21:58Z",
      "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
      "body": "> Considering all that, I'd say that the options are\n\nI think option 2 or 3 makes the most sense. The current design seems to have been introduced in https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/pull/15402 which broke up cs_main locking. I think this was done to make it safely interruptible, limit unbounded growth of the scheduler queue causing high memory usage, and allow other threads to acquire cs_main. [This comment](https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/issues/9027#issuecomment-1291102752) by @willcl-ark seems to indicate that high memory usage is still present with really deep `invalidateblock` calls, so maybe we don't need to worry about that if we end up locking cs_main for longer.\n\nAlso, this is just an rpc call so perhaps we could add a disclaimer saying that asserts may happen in unexpected scenarios?",
      "user": {
